00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.140
hey guys my name is Amy Blea on the
offering team here with IBM cloud and

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.460
today I want to talk to you about some
traditional types of storage file and

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.780
block storage kind of give you an
overview of what they are and then give

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.529
you some tips on how to decide when to
use one over the other so let's get

00:00:16.529 --> 00:00:22.199
started with block storage block storage
is storage where data is written and raw

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:28.320
blocks on the storage and it's accessed
by the servers that you have through a

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.360
storage area network so you have all of
your servers they can either be on the

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.750
same network with each other and a
different network but they all connect

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:42.420
through the storage through this storage
area network now some of the advantages

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.739
of using block storage is that block
storage provides the lowest possible

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:53.190
latency for your applications and you
can also use it for those applications

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:58.949
that require high performance or lots of
AI ops another thing you'll find with

00:00:58.949 --> 00:01:04.739
block storage is that in general it is
highly redundant most block storage

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:10.320
services provide a capability that's
built-in where your data is redundant

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.780
across the volume so that if so there
should be either a volume should go down

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:21.540
or a disk should go away and you can
recover your data from another place

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:27.200
without having any kind of impact to
your application now file storage is

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.030
connected to your service a little
differently than block storages

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:38.280
everything is connected on the same
network so you have all of your files or

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:46.320
your file share here those are all files
and they can all be accessed on the same

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.810
network by any of the servers on that
network so it's network attached storage

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:56.490
file storage is highly scalable so you
can have a multiple file shares on your

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:00.509
network and have all of your servers
attached to that at one time it's

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:05.159
accessible to multiple runtime so here
in this illustration we have a single

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:09.060
file share that has multiple servers
accessing it all at once and you can

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.790
also have multiple simultaneous reads
and writes going to

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:17.780
your file share all at one time without
having to worry about your data being

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:23.569
overwritten so how do you know when is
the right time to choose either block

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:26.239
storage or file storage for your
application

00:02:26.239 --> 00:02:30.200
well first of all you need to think
about what you're using it for so for

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:36.519
example let's say you have a VMware
configuration where you have multiple

00:02:36.519 --> 00:02:41.690
virtual servers with VMware on them and
you need boot volumes well in that case

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:48.530
you would use block storage if you have
workloads such as transactional

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:52.340
databases or relational databases that
require very low latency and high

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:58.670
performance you would choose block
storage in situations where you have a

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:04.250
mix of structured and unstructured data
for example a web hosting server where

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:09.470
you have both you know text files as
well as media files then you would

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:16.340
choose file storage and then if you have
a collaborative space where you need to

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:21.889
have multiple users accessing it all at
one time working together doing you know

00:03:21.889 --> 00:03:29.239
reads and writes all at the same time
you would choose a file storage so like

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:33.410
I mentioned in the beginning block and
file storage are very traditional types

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:38.410
of storage they're not as flashy and
sunshiny and exciting as some of the new

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:43.400
storage services that have entered the
market but they are still very relevant

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.030
and useful for the different types of
workloads that you have whether it's on

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.540
premise or in the cloud thanks for
checking out this video and the

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.870
differences between file and block
storage with me today and if you have

